"Modern physics lab",
"{#} to {#} minutes (a full lab period with the table and fit)",
"Electrons accelerated through V have a de Broglie wavelength λ = h/p, with p = √(2meV) (plus a small relativistic correction). At a few kilovolts λ is about {#} pm, comparable to the spacing of atoms in a crystal.",
"A thin film of graphite is made of many tiny crystals at random angles. For each set of lattice planes, some crystals sit at the Bragg angle, 2d sin θ = λ, so the electrons leave on a cone at {#}θ and make a ring on the screen.",
"Two rings mean two plane spacings, d₁ = {#} nm and d₂ = {#} nm. Their ratio is √{#}, set by the hexagonal lattice, and it does not change with V.",
"Raising V shortens λ and shrinks the rings as {#}/√V: the particle's momentum sets its wavelength.",
"Students should know Bragg's law (from X-ray diffraction or a grating) and the energy and momentum of a particle. Decide whether students will use the caliper (straight across, the chord) or the ruler on the glass (along the curve, the arc). Measure your own tube's graphite to screen distance L if you can: the maker gives {#} mm, but handouts quote {#} or {#} mm, and the value goes straight into d. Turn on <strong>Blind</strong> before projecting the table.",
