"<strong>Raise the voltage.</strong> The rings shrink. Ask for the scaling before anyone measures (Q3).",
"<strong>Two rings, two spacings.</strong> Use the cross section: each ring is a cone at its own Bragg angle. The ratio of the ring diameters stays near {#} at every voltage.",
"<strong>Is it the electrons?</strong> Bring the magnet near the neck: the whole pattern moves. X-rays would not be deflected.",
"<strong>Why only two rings.</strong> The graphite sheet with its two families of rows of atoms, {#} nm and {#} nm apart.",
"<strong>Measure the spacings.</strong> Six simulated readings fill the table; students add their own, choose exact or small angle geometry, and compare the fitted d with the accepted values.",
