"<strong>\"Each electron makes a ring.\"</strong> Each electron lands at one point. The rings are where many electrons are likely to land, built up from very many hits.",
"<strong>\"The rings come from electrons bouncing off atoms like balls.\"</strong> Classical scattering from random atoms gives a smooth blur. Sharp rings at angles set by λ and d are interference, a wave property.",
"<strong>\"Higher voltage makes bigger rings.\"</strong> Faster electrons have shorter wavelengths, so smaller Bragg angles and smaller rings.",
"<strong>\"The two rings are first and second order of one spacing.\"</strong> Second order of {#} nm would sit at twice the angle, not at the observed ratio of √{#}. They are first order from two different plane spacings.",
"λ = h/√(2meV({#} + eV/(2mc²))). The relativistic correction shortens λ by about {#} to {#}% between {#} and {#} kV.",
"Bragg: 2d sin θ = λ; each ring is the cone at {#}θ to the beam. The demo finds exactly where each cone meets the spherical screen (L = {#} mm from graphite to screen, bulb radius {#} mm). The caliper reads the straight chord across the ring, the ruler the arc along the glass.",
